# How Executable Files Work (Windows XP / NT)

This explains how `.exe` files work on Windows, from the binary format
on disk to a running process in memory, based on the Windows NT architecture in Windows XP.

---

## 1. The PE (Portable Executable) Format

Every `.exe` (and `.dll`, `.sys`, `.ocx`) on Windows uses the **PE format**.
A PE file is a structured binary with headers, sections, and metadata that tells
the OS loader exactly how to map the file into memory and start execution.

### File Layout on Disk

```
Offset 0x00    ┌──────────────────────────┐
               │   DOS Header (64 bytes)  │  <- IMAGE_DOS_HEADER
               │   e_magic = "MZ" (0x5A4D)│
               │   e_lfanew = offset to PE│
               ├──────────────────────────┤
               │   DOS Stub Program       │  <- "This program cannot be
               │   (optional, ~100 bytes) │     run in DOS mode."
               ├──────────────────────────┤
e_lfanew ───►  │   PE Signature (4 bytes) │  <- "PE\0\0" (0x00004550)
               ├──────────────────────────┤
               │   COFF File Header       │  <- IMAGE_FILE_HEADER (20 bytes)
               │   - Machine type (x86)   │
               │   - NumberOfSections     │
               │   - TimeDateStamp        │
               │   - SizeOfOptionalHeader │
               │   - Characteristics      │
               ├──────────────────────────┤
               │   Optional Header        │  <- IMAGE_OPTIONAL_HEADER (224 bytes for PE32)
               │   - Magic (0x10B = PE32) │
               │   - AddressOfEntryPoint  │
               │   - ImageBase(0x00400000)|
               │   - SectionAlignment     │
               │   - FileAlignment        │
               │   - SizeOfImage          │
               │   - SizeOfHeaders        │
               │   - Subsystem (GUI/CUI)  │
               │   - DataDirectory[16]    │
               ├──────────────────────────┤
               │   Section Headers[]      │  <- IMAGE_SECTION_HEADER (40 bytes each)
               │   .text  - code          │
               │   .rdata - read-only data│
               │   .data  - read/write data│
               │   .rsrc  - resources     │
               │   .reloc - relocations   │
               ├──────────────────────────┤
               │   Section Bodies         │  <- Raw data for each section,
               │   (aligned to FileAlign.)│     padded to FileAlignment
               └──────────────────────────┘
```

### Key Structures (from winnt.h)

```c
// The very first bytes of any PE file
typedef struct _IMAGE_DOS_HEADER {
    WORD  e_magic;      // 0x5A4D ("MZ") - Mark Zbikowski's initials
    WORD  e_cblp;       // Bytes on last page of file
    // ... (legacy DOS fields) ...
    LONG  e_lfanew;     // Offset to IMAGE_NT_HEADERS (the PE header)
} IMAGE_DOS_HEADER;

// The main PE header, found at offset e_lfanew
typedef struct _IMAGE_NT_HEADERS {
    DWORD                 Signature;       // 0x00004550 ("PE\0\0")
    IMAGE_FILE_HEADER     FileHeader;      // Machine, section count, etc.
    IMAGE_OPTIONAL_HEADER OptionalHeader;  // Entry point, image base, etc.
} IMAGE_NT_HEADERS;

// Describes one section (.text, .data, etc.)
typedef struct _IMAGE_SECTION_HEADER {
    BYTE  Name[8];              // ".text", ".data", etc.
    DWORD VirtualSize;          // Size in memory
    DWORD VirtualAddress;       // RVA (Relative Virtual Address) when loaded
    DWORD SizeOfRawData;        // Size on disk
    DWORD PointerToRawData;     // File offset to raw data
    DWORD Characteristics;      // Flags: readable, writable, executable
} IMAGE_SECTION_HEADER;
```

### Data Directories

The Optional Header contains an array of 16 **Data Directories**, each pointing
to important metadata:

| Index | Name                    | Purpose                                    |
|-------|-------------------------|--------------------------------------------|
| 0     | Export Table            | Functions exported by this module           |
| 1     | **Import Table**        | DLLs and functions this module needs        |
| 2     | Resource Table          | Icons, strings, dialogs, version info       |
| 3     | Exception Table         | SEH exception handler data                  |
| 4     | Certificate Table       | Authenticode digital signatures             |
| 5     | **Base Relocation Table** | Fixups if loaded at non-preferred address |
| 6     | Debug Directory         | Debug info (PDB path, checksum)             |
| 12    | **IAT**                 | Import Address Table (resolved at load)     |

