## 3. How Code Actually Executes

### The .text Section

The `.text` section contains the compiled machine code. For x86 (Windows XP):

```
; A simple function call in x86 assembly:
; Calling CreateFileW through the IAT
push  0                          ; hTemplateFile
push  FILE_ATTRIBUTE_NORMAL      ; dwFlagsAndAttributes
push  OPEN_EXISTING              ; dwCreationDisposition
push  0                          ; lpSecurityAttributes
push  0                          ; dwShareMode
push  GENERIC_READ               ; dwDesiredAccess
push  offset szFileName          ; lpFileName
call  dword ptr [__imp__CreateFileW]  ; indirect call through IAT
; EAX now contains the file handle (or INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE)
```

The CPU executes these instructions sequentially. When `call` is executed:
1. Push the return address onto the stack
2. Jump to the target address (the real `CreateFileW` in kernel32.dll)
3. kernel32 does its work, eventually making a syscall (`int 0x2e` or
   `sysenter`) to transition to kernel mode
4. The kernel performs the actual I/O operation
5. Returns back to user mode → back to kernel32 → `ret` back to your code

### System Calls

When user-mode code needs kernel services (file I/O, memory allocation, etc.),
it goes through ntdll.dll's system call stubs:

```
; ntdll!NtCreateFile (system call stub)
mov   eax, 0x25          ; syscall number for NtCreateFile
mov   edx, 0x7FFE0300    ; SystemCallStub pointer
call  dword ptr [edx]    ; -> KiFastSystemCall (uses SYSENTER)
ret   0x2C
; KiFastSystemCall:
mov   edx, esp
sysenter                 ; transition to kernel mode
```

